#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087497 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "07d823d0-39f8-5d70-8df4-7fd15783e148" :question/math-academy-id "e-5427" :question/problem "Are the following transformations linear?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\nT\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} = kT\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},; T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix} = T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + T\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_1$. Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}(kx)^{2} \\\\ (kx)(ky)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}k^{2}x^{2} \\\\ k^{2}xy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= k\\begin{bmatrix}kx^{2} \\\\ kxy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\ne kT_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_1$ is not a linear transformation.\n\nNow, let's consider $\\mathbf{T}_2$.\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n$T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}kx \\\\ ky\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}3kx \\\\ kx + 2ky\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}k(3x) \\\\ k(x + 2y)\\end{bmatrix}; = k\\begin{bmatrix}3x \\\\ x + 2y\\end{bmatrix}; = kT_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.✓$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n$T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} \\\\ y_{1} + y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}3(x_{1} + x_{2}) \\\\ (x_{1} + x_{2}) + 2(y_{1} + y_{2})\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{1} + 3x_{2} \\\\ (x_{1} + 2y_{1}) + (x_{2} + 2y_{2})\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{1} \\\\ x_{1} + 2y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} + 2y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}; = T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ y_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + T_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{2} \\\\ y_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.✓$\n\nTherefore, $\\textbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/id #uuid "8fe1f455-e739-59cb-95c2-0378dff32b8e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5428" :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation and $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})\n$$\n\nNoting that $\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}) &= T(6\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) = 6T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "e34b53de-72d8-541f-bd04-16d2cbf1e6b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5429" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. If $\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)$." :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT(kv + w) = T(kv) + T(w) = kT(v) + T(w)\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(4v_{1} + v_{2}) &= 4T(v_{1}) + T(v_{2}) \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} &= 4T(v_{1}) + \\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n4T(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n4T(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{1}) &= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-32 \\\\ 36\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nT(v_{1}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fbd335c-28cc-5f02-b773-f685e8b15417" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Sum Under a Map Using Vector Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "fc3159fb-751c-55d6-b41e-4744b80b0ae9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5430" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$. If $\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ then find $\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)$." :question/worked-solution "First, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 + \\mathbf{v}_2=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17v_{1} + 12v_{2} &= 5v_{1} + 12v_{1} + 12v_{2} \\\\\n&= 5v_{1} + 12(v_{1} + v_{2}) \\\\\n&= 5v_{1} + 12(0) \\\\\n&= 5v_{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(17\\mathbf{v}_1+12\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(5\\mathbf{v}_1)\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(k\\mathbf{v})=k\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(5v_{1}) &= 5T(v_{1}) \\\\\n&= 5\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, $\\begin{bmatrix}45 \\\\ -60 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad1c29f7-c0e7-5bed-944f-a2e33579c7b8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of a Vector Under a Map Using the Scalar Multiplication Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b65ae02-3f1f-5cea-a342-4884c02d8150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085490} {:answer/id #uuid "96ab548f-58fb-5c75-a69c-bf29d516a899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085491} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b516ee-46b5-5944-bfd3-b8a8715f7bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085492} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8ddc48-f5a9-5b82-a3be-cab008ac9e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085493} {:answer/id #uuid "77f98240-514e-52b1-a9f6-c995050dd33d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96ab548f-58fb-5c75-a69c-bf29d516a899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085491} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf57478-9b43-5c2f-8ec7-b7abe1415531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085489}] :question/id #uuid "95ad2111-91ad-555b-a492-6ecabaa23b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-37903" :question/problem "If $T$ is a linear transformation and $T(\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}) = \\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}$, then find $T(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})$."}]}
